From 796dc3a96b95faf03a576ff309c820de7cc3de8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duckfamily <156525766+duckfamily@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 02:32:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 02/13] Harden the process and protect auth keys in memory

Applies extension-point disable, image load restrictions, heap termination
on corruption, Arbitrary Code Guard and Code Integrity Guard, as the very
first statement in main() so the policies are in place before anything else
runs. TDESKTOP_ACG=0 and TDESKTOP_CIG=0 are escape hatches for a machine
where a driver disagrees.

CIG needs no code signing certificate: it governs images loaded after the
policy is applied, and our own executable is already mapped by then. All
130 modules this build loads were checked and every one is Microsoft
signed.

The source comments record which policies were deliberately left alone -
child process, strict handle check, a restrictive DACL - and why, so nobody
adds them expecting an improvement.

AuthKey locks its pages against paging and wipes them with OPENSSL_cleanse
in the destructor, because the compiler may elide a write that is never
read again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
